Relative Strength Index (RSI) is another key indicator,
measuring the speed and change of price movements. RSI
moves between zero and 100 and can signal to traders
whether an asset is overbought or oversold. Typically, a
reading above 70 suggests overbought conditions, while a
reading below 30 suggests oversold conditions. These
insights are critical as they can guide traders in making
timely entry and exit decisions.
In addition to indicators, chart patterns are a fundamental
aspect of technical analysis. These patterns, formed by the
price movements on a chart, can signal continuation or
reversal trends. For example, a Head and Shoulders pattern is
considered a reliable reversal pattern, indicating that an
upward trend is likely to come to an end. Conversely, a Cup
and Handle pattern can signal a continuation of a bullish
trend after a consolidation phase.
Beyond these basics, traders often make use of support and
resistance levels to enhance their analysis. Support levels
indicate where a price tends to find a lower bound, while
resistance levels show where it tends to find an upper bound.
